WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.680
We've never gotten a big-screen version of
one of Spidey's most famous challenges: the

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.760
nefarious Sinister Six.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.580
Or have we?

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.870
The Sinister Six might already exist in the
MCU, even if you've never noticed.

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:19.030
If you're not familiar with them, the Sinister
Six have a history with Spidey that dates

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.520
back all the way to 1964.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.720
While every issue advanced the superhero soap
opera that was the Marvel Universe back then,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.100
the publishing schedule was punctuated by
special oversized issues that, because they

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.240
were meant to be a once-a-year spectacular,
were called Annuals.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.180
Across the street at DC Comics, Annuals were
pretty much just big reprints designed to

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.380
get "classic" stories into the hands of readers
who had missed them the first time around,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.580
often built around a theme.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.940
At Marvel, things were done a little differently.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.920
While their Annuals did feature reprints of
key stories, the lead story in every issue

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.679
was not only all-new material, it was also
something meant to be a pretty big deal.

00:00:56.679 --> 00:01:01.890
The Fantastic Four Annuals gave readers an
origin for Doctor Doom, a war between Atlantis

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:06.510
and the surface world, and the wedding of
Reed Richards and Sue Storm, an oversized

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.570
epic that guest-starred the entire Marvel
Universe circa 1965, including Stan Lee and

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.960
Jack Kirby themselves.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.940
For the first Spider-Man Annual, the idea
that Stan Lee and Steve Ditko came up with

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.420
was one of the simplest ways to make a story
feel important: if one supervillain could

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.360
make a regular issue seem like a big deal,
then why not throw in a few more?

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.910
In fact, why not take virtually every Spider-Man
villain who had been created over the past

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.960
year and throw them all against Spidey in
one massive all-out brawl?

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.280
When it hit the stands in Amazing Spider-Man
Annual #1, "The Sinister Six" clocked in at

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:44.980
41 pages, and contained some of Lee and Ditko's
best work ever.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.630
The idea was simple: after escaping from prison,
Doctor Octopus gathered five villains who

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:55.280
wanted revenge on Spider-Man Electro, the
Vulture, Mysterio, the Sandman and Kraven

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.060
the Hunter.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.060
After figuring out that Spider-Man had some
kind of connection to them, Doc Ock kidnapped

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.580
Betty Brant and Aunt May, leaving behind clues
that would lead Spidey from one villain to

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.080
the next.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.200
It was basically a scavenger hunt, but with
a strong chance of being mauled by leopards.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:15.590
Ditko punctuated each of the battles with
a full-page splash of Spidey taking on the

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.040
bad guys, which was something that wasn't
actually seen that often back then.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:24.000
The editors of the '60s figured kids would
feel ripped off if each page wasn't crammed

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.020
full of story, but it's hard to imagine any
fan looking at a massive shot of Spider-Man

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:33.290
crashing through a wall to punch out Mysterio
while threatening to "play a drum solo on

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.580
that glass fishbowl" and think it was anything
but awesome.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.560
Needless to say, the story was a hit, and
that original lineup with Otto Octavius as

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.329
the mastermind would go down in Marvel history
as one of the greatest villainous teams ever

00:02:46.329 --> 00:02:46.820
assembled.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.740
Surprisingly, the Sinister Six didn't make
another appearance for a while.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.670
It wasn't until 1990 that we got the team's
proper return in a story appropriately called

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.440
"The Return of the Sinister Six."

00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:02.719
The only big difference was the addition of
the Hobgoblin to the roster, replacing the

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.680
temporarily deceased Kraven.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.700
Unlike the original straightforward tale,
however, the return was full-on bonkers.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.409
Not only did it see the death of Aunt May's
elderly boyfriend Nathan, but it was built

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:19.370
around Doc Ock manipulating the other villains
into a world domination plot and then revealing

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:24.940
an insane twist: he was really launching a
satellite that would extort wealthy addicts

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.400
by causing them to painfully convulse whenever
they did cocaine.

00:03:28.609 --> 00:03:32.720
Fortunately, with the help of a face turn
from the Sandman, Spider-Man was able to defeat

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.040
Doc Ock and make the world safe for cocaine
once again.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.371
That story cemented the Sinister Six as the
Marvel Universe's premiere villain team, and

00:03:41.371 --> 00:03:44.529
paved the way for them to branch out in more
ways than one.

00:03:44.529 --> 00:03:48.580
They've appeared as a team in multiple Spider-Man
cartoons, as well as video games from the

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.720
NES all the way to 2018's PS4 Spider-Man.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.520
They're even in Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark,
the broadway musical!

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:00.680
The only place they haven't been yet is the
movie theater… unless they've actually been

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:01.779
there all along.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.269
If the Sinister Six do show up in the MCU,
it's likely that it won't be the classic lineup

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:12.740
that was introduced back in Amazing Spider-Man
Annual #1, for one simple reason.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.859
Movies tend to shy away from using the same
villains that have been seen before with other

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:17.960
versions of the character.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.100
Before the MCU, half of the original roster
had already shown up in Spider-Man movies.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:27.710
Doctor Octopus, played by Alfred Molina rocking
the rarely seen "two trench coats at the same

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.280
time" look, was in Spider-Man 2.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.020
The Sandman appeared alongside Venom and a
dancing Peter Parker in Spider-Man 3.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.620
2014's Amazing Spider-Man 2 brought in Jamie
Foxx as Electro, but it's okay if you forgot

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:40.340
about that one.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.920
"What's your name again?

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.160
How could you forget me?

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.620
Oh I do I know it, I know it, don't tell me.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:46.120
It's Max.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:46.720
Is it Max?"

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.140
As far as the other half of the classic lineup
goes, the Vulture was the primary villain

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.980
of Spider-Man: Homecoming, and Mysterio is
in Spider-Man: Far from Home.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.030
The only one who's missing entirely is Kraven
the Hunter, but that's not a huge problem.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.650
There are more Spider-Man villains floating
around the Marvel Cinematic Universe who could

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:04.950
be used in his place.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:11.129
In the span of a single film, Spider-Man managed
to encounter more of his classic foes than

00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:13.080
Iron Man did in ten years.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.090
The obvious one, of course, was the Vulture,
but he's just the tip of the villainous iceberg.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:21.920
On the off chance that you missed it, take
a closer look at Adrian Toomes and the cabal

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.110
of high-tech arms dealers that used to be
his construction crew, and you'll see both

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:31.640
the Shocker and the Tinkerer who debuted alongside
the Vulture back in Amazing Spider-Man #2.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.740
There's also an appearance by the Prowler
and the Scorpion.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:39.840
Thanks to Into the Spider-Verse, those two
wound up being two unlikely picks for Spider-Foes

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.680
who would both make it into two different
movie universes in the span of 18 months.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:49.050
That's five major Spider-Man villains well,
four major villains and the Tinkerer, really

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.680
and they're just the ones we know for sure.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.500
Peter has a science teacher named "Ms. Warren"
in Homecoming who might very well turn out

00:05:55.509 --> 00:06:00.199
to be the MCU's version of the Jackal, who
in the comics is Peter's old science teacher,

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:00.980
Miles Warren.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.039
Of course, that's just if, for some reason,
they want to do a movie about Spider-Clones

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:09.379
in spite of everyone who was reading Spider-Man
comics in the '90s begging them not to.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.280
Generally speaking, superhero movies have
a villain problem.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:18.200
Not only do the villains tend to just be palette-swapped
versions of the good guys, but they also have

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.140
an unfortunate tendency to die after a single
film.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:25.520
Even when the villains are interesting and
compelling in their own right, like Erik Killmonger

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.800
in Black Panther, they usually only last for
a single film before they're killed off.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.640
Or they used to be, until Homecoming.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.740
Admittedly, there were MCU villains who stuck
around before that, including Helmut Zemo

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.800
in Captain America: Civil War and Baron Mordo
in Doctor Strange.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:46.340
Thanos also appeared in a handful of movies,
although he managed to die twice in the last

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.960
one, which kind of makes up for it.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.900
Think about other major Marvel villains, though.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:54.259
Ultron, whose entire deal is that he comes
back deadlier every time he's defeated, was

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:57.320
thoroughly obliterated at the end of the film
that bears his name.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:01.940
Ego the Living Planet was blown up after fighting
a giant holographic Pac-Man.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:06.510
These are powerhouse villains in the comics
who just cease to exist in the films.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:07.990
And that makes sense, too.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:13.210
As much as the MCU films form an ongoing universe,
they're not made for the perpetual publication

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:17.170
schedule that comics have, so there's no real
reason to keep the bad guys around if you

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:19.700
think you can tell a more compelling story
by killing them.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:24.260
Once again, Killmonger is the prime example
of a villain whose death is meant to mean

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.480
something larger than just the end of the
film.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.110
The thing is, when that's the default setting
that every movie goes with, it stands out

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:32.240
when the villains don't die.

00:07:32.669 --> 00:07:36.900
Loki, for instance, has stabbed his way into
the hearts of moviegoers across the world

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.860
precisely because he just keeps surviving
to get another one over on Thor.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:42.580
"Surpriiiise."

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:47.860
It becomes a universe where characters only
stick around if, like Loki, there are plans

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:49.000
for them in the future.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.100
That brings us, again, back to Spider-Man:
Homecoming.

00:07:53.289 --> 00:07:57.500
It makes a point of keeping its villain around,
and only partly because rescuing the bad guy

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:01.240
says a lot about who Peter Parker is and why
he does what he does.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.900
That leads to the epilogue in prison, where
the Vulture encounters Mac Gargan.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:10.440
On the surface, it feels like another subversion
of what we expect from the superhero movie

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:15.100
formula: when he's given the chance to assemble
a team of villains and reveal Parker's identity,

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:16.720
Toomes doesn't take it.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.860
"If I knew who he was, he'd already be dead."

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.020
Whether it's because he's not the bad guy
he pretends to be when he threatens Peter

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.840
in the car which seems unlikely because he
buries him under a few tons of warehouse cement

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.909
immediately after or because he realizes he
owes Spider-Man his life, he gives up on the

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:36.640
opportunity.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.300
The scene shows us something else, though.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:42.560
The fact that Gargan is still around and trying
to make connections with other people who

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:47.190
want revenge against Spider-Man shows that
the groundwork is already there for a sinister

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:47.820
team-up.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.560
If nothing else, Spider-Man is the first hero
in the entirety of the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.130
to have six enemies who are both mad at him
and still alive.

00:08:57.649 --> 00:09:02.510
All of this is, of course, pure speculation,
but it's pretty easy to look at a movie where

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:06.950
the bad guys are kept around and assume that
this is all building to something big.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:09.820
It's also pretty easy to imagine how it could
go down.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:14.660
Hydro-Man fits pretty easily into the same
role as the Sandman, without the baggage of

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:18.970
being in one of the worst-received Spider-Man
movies, and the same goes for the Shocker

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:20.120
replacing Electro.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:25.500
As much as fans would love to see his crop-top
lion vest, Kraven could be easily substituted

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.300
with a revenge-seeking Lizard.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.980
And if the Vulture truly is done with his
Spidey-fighting days, the Jackal could conceivably

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.480
fill the role of the older science weirdo.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:39.820
Throw in the Tinkerer as the wild card-slash-tech
guy, and that's a pretty solid Sinister Six.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.680
And again: those are just the characters we
know about.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:45.920
Who knows what villains Marvel might pull
from the comics in the future.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:49.340
Who's to say they won't toss in Stegron the
Dinosaur Man for good measure?

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:54.160
From a storytelling standpoint, building to
the Sinister Six might not even be necessary.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.860
As much as we love to imagine Spider-Man as
the everyman hero who balances his superheroic

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:03.560
life with his everyday problems, that requires
a Spider-Man who's always struggling against

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:04.060
the odds.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:08.779
Meanwhile, the cinematic Peter Parker has
crammed a whole lot of living into a very

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:09.440
short time.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:14.420
In the three years since he was introduced,
the MCU's Spider-Man has gone toe-to-toe with

00:10:14.421 --> 00:10:19.620
Captain America, rocketed off to outer space,
fought Thanos on the other side of the universe,

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:23.790
spent five years as a pile of dust before
being resurrected from the dead, and fought

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:28.740
Thanos again on Earth alongside dozens of
other superheroes, including at least two

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:29.880
literal gods.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.320
It's kind of hard to go back to just fighting
the Rhino after that.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:38.700
The Rhino and five of his very angry buddies,
on the other hand, sounds like the right kind

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:39.200
of challenge.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:41.780
